 "notes": "Wikidata (CC0 1.0) through the public SPARQL endpoint https://query.wikidata.org/sparql, queried on 2026-10-27, one query at a time and at most one per minute (the endpoint's current rate limit), every query with a LIMIT; each file here is one query's raw JSON result, unmodified, and its URL holds the full query text. Wikidata is edited continuously: a fresh fetch on another machine or day can return different rows, so the build reads these saved files. Fact kinds: birth_year, city_country, capital, work_author, work_year, film_director, founding_year, mountain_range, opera_composer. Only best-rank (truthy) statements are used and only subjects with exactly ONE value for the property; time values need year precision or better (day-precision dates on 1-12 January before 1924 are dropped because of the Julian-to-Gregorian conversion). Subjects need an English label; subjects whose label repeats within their kind (birth year: label plus description) are dropped as ambiguous. Nobel laureates (any rank of P166 for the six Nobel prizes) are never subjects and no text mentions Nobel (the dev set's source is the Nobel Prize API). Distractors: years = four distinct years within the gold +-1..15 (the gold's rank among the options is uniform); entities = four values of the same kind from the same continent / country / era first, weighted by how often a value is a gold answer, never sharing or nesting wording with the gold or the subject, and for mountain ranges never an ancestor or descendant range of the gold (gold and distractor ranges are leaf ranges, i.e. no other range in the data lies inside them). Group key (source_id) = the subject QID; split = sha256 of the QID; one fact kind per subject; per split each kind is filled from interleaved sitelink terciles (popular to obscure), one gold value at most 3 % of a kind, no kind above 24 % of a split."
